issue.description:
Two versions of an API module exist: v1 uses old patterns (dict returns,
no response models, string status codes) and v2 uses current best practices
(Pydantic response models, proper HTTP status codes, typed responses).
Your task is to read both files and write a migration guide in Markdown
that documents every difference with before/after code examples.

Step 1 - Create api/v1/endpoints.py with 5 endpoints using old patterns:

  from fastapi import APIRouter
  router = APIRouter(prefix="/v1")

  # 1. Returns a plain dict — no response model
  @router.get("/users/{user_id}")
  def get_user(user_id: int):
      return {"id": user_id, "name": "Alice", "email": "alice@example.com"}

  # 2. Returns plain dict on create — uses 200 instead of 201
  @router.post("/users")
  def create_user(name: str, email: str):
      return {"id": 1, "name": name, "email": email}

  # 3. Returns string "not found" on missing resource — no HTTPException
  @router.get("/items/{item_id}")
  def get_item(item_id: int):
      if item_id > 100:
          return "not found"
      return {"id": item_id, "title": "Widget"}

  # 4. Delete returns 200 with a message dict — should be 204 with no body
  @router.delete("/users/{user_id}")
  def delete_user(user_id: int):
      return {"message": "deleted"}

  # 5. List endpoint returns raw list — no envelope or metadata
  @router.get("/users")
  def list_users():
      return [{"id": 1, "name": "Alice"}, {"id": 2, "name": "Bob"}]

Step 2 - Create api/v2/endpoints.py with the same 5 endpoints using
current FastAPI best practices:

  from fastapi import APIRouter, HTTPException, status
  from pydantic import BaseModel

  class UserOut(BaseModel):
      id: int
      name: str
      email: str

  class UserIn(BaseModel):
      name: str
      email: str

  class ItemOut(BaseModel):
      id: int
      title: str

  class UsersListOut(BaseModel):
      items: list[UserOut]
      total: int

  router = APIRouter(prefix="/v2")

  # 1. Typed response model
  @router.get("/users/{user_id}", response_model=UserOut)
  def get_user(user_id: int) -> UserOut:
      return UserOut(id=user_id, name="Alice", email="alice@example.com")

  # 2. 201 Created with response model
  @router.post("/users", response_model=UserOut, status_code=status.HTTP_201_CREATED)
  def create_user(body: UserIn) -> UserOut:
      return UserOut(id=1, name=body.name, email=body.email)

  # 3. HTTPException for not found
  @router.get("/items/{item_id}", response_model=ItemOut)
  def get_item(item_id: int) -> ItemOut:
      if item_id > 100:
          raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail="Item not found")
      return ItemOut(id=item_id, title="Widget")

  # 4. 204 No Content on delete
  @router.delete("/users/{user_id}", status_code=status.HTTP_204_NO_CONTENT)
  def delete_user(user_id: int) -> None:
      return None

  # 5. Envelope with metadata
  @router.get("/users", response_model=UsersListOut)
  def list_users() -> UsersListOut:
      users = [UserOut(id=1, name="Alice", email="alice@example.com"),
               UserOut(id=2, name="Bob", email="bob@example.com")]
      return UsersListOut(items=users, total=len(users))

Step 3 - Write docs/migration_guide.md that documents every difference
between v1 and v2. The guide must:
  - Have an introductory section explaining why these patterns changed
  - Cover all 5 endpoints with a dedicated section per endpoint
  - Show the v1 code (before) and v2 code (after) for each endpoint
  - Explain WHY each change matters (not just what changed):
      * response_model: enables automatic serialization, validation,
        and OpenAPI schema generation
      * 201 vs 200 on create: semantically correct, tells clients the
        resource was created
      * HTTPException vs string return: clients can check status codes
        without parsing the body
      * 204 on delete: no-content signals success without a body,
        reduces payload
      * response envelope: pagination metadata travels with the list
  - All code examples must be syntactically valid Python
  - Use fenced code blocks with language tags (```python)

Step 4 - Create scripts/check_guide_syntax.py that accepts a markdown
file path as sys.argv[1], extracts all fenced python code blocks, parses
each with ast.parse, and exits 0 if all parse successfully or 1 with an
error message on failure.

Step 5 - Create tests/test_migration_guide.py that verifies:
  - docs/migration_guide.md exists
  - The guide mentions all 5 endpoint paths (/users/{user_id}, /users,
    /items/{item_id}, POST /users, DELETE /users/{user_id})
  - The guide contains at least 5 fenced python code blocks
  - Each endpoint section contains both v1 and v2 code examples
    (check that each section includes at least one "v1" and one "v2"
    label or "before" / "after" heading)
  - The Python code blocks are syntactically valid (invoke
    scripts/check_guide_syntax.py with subprocess and assert returncode 0)

You have a legacy utility module with 5 functions that have no type
annotations. Your task is to add complete, correct type annotations to
all 5 functions so that mypy passes without errors.

Step 1 — Create the file legacy/utils.py with these 5 functions (no
annotations, exactly as shown):

  def process_data(items, multiplier):
      return [x * multiplier for x in items]

  def validate_input(value, min_val, max_val):
      return min_val <= value <= max_val

  def format_output(data, prefix, separator):
      return separator.join(f"{prefix}{item}" for item in data)

  def merge_configs(base, override):
      result = dict(base)
      result.update(override)
      return result

  def parse_date(date_str, fmt):
      from datetime import datetime
      return datetime.strptime(date_str, fmt)

Step 2 — Create the file tests/test_legacy_utils.py that imports and
calls all 5 functions:

  from legacy.utils import process_data, validate_input, format_output, merge_configs, parse_date

  def test_process_data():
      assert process_data([1, 2, 3], 2) == [2, 4, 6]

  def test_validate_input():
      assert validate_input(5, 1, 10) is True
      assert validate_input(0, 1, 10) is False

  def test_format_output():
      assert format_output(["a", "b"], "x_", ", ") == "x_a, x_b"

  def test_merge_configs():
      assert merge_configs({"a": 1}, {"b": 2}) == {"a": 1, "b": 2}

  def test_parse_date():
      from datetime import datetime
      result = parse_date("2024-01-15", "%Y-%m-%d")
      assert result == datetime(2024, 1, 15)

Step 3 — Add type annotations to all 5 functions in legacy/utils.py.
The annotations must match the actual usage in the tests above. Use
modern Python 3.10+ syntax (list[int] not List[int]).

Requirements:
- All 5 functions must have annotated parameters and return types.
- mypy legacy/utils.py must pass with no errors.
- All tests in tests/test_legacy_utils.py must pass.
- Do not change the logic of any function, only add annotations.

A legacy test suite uses the unittest style. Modernize it to idiomatic
pytest while preserving all 20 test cases.

Step 1 — Create tests/test_old_style.py with exactly 20 tests using the
unittest style. Use this exact content:

  import unittest

  # --- helpers used by tests ---
  def add(a, b): return a + b
  def divide(a, b): return a / b
  def is_even(n): return n % 2 == 0
  def to_upper(s): return s.upper()
  def reverse(lst): return lst[::-1]

  class TestMath(unittest.TestCase):
      def setUp(self):
          self.x = 10
          self.y = 3

      def tearDown(self):
          pass

      def test_add_positive(self):
          self.assertEqual(add(self.x, self.y), 13)

      def test_add_zero(self):
          self.assertEqual(add(self.x, 0), self.x)

      def test_add_negative(self):
          self.assertEqual(add(self.x, -self.y), 7)

      def test_divide_exact(self):
          self.assertEqual(divide(9, 3), 3.0)

      def test_divide_by_zero(self):
          self.assertRaises(ZeroDivisionError, divide, self.x, 0)

      def test_is_even_true(self):
          self.assertTrue(is_even(self.x))

      def test_is_even_false(self):
          self.assertFalse(is_even(self.y))

  class TestStrings(unittest.TestCase):
      def setUp(self):
          self.word = "hello"

      def test_upper(self):
          self.assertEqual(to_upper(self.word), "HELLO")

      def test_upper_empty(self):
          self.assertEqual(to_upper(""), "")

      def test_upper_already(self):
          self.assertEqual(to_upper("WORLD"), "WORLD")

      def test_upper_mixed(self):
          self.assertEqual(to_upper("hElLo"), "HELLO")

  class TestList(unittest.TestCase):
      def setUp(self):
          self.nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

      def test_reverse_list(self):
          self.assertEqual(reverse(self.nums), [5, 4, 3, 2, 1])

      def test_reverse_single(self):
          self.assertEqual(reverse([42]), [42])

      def test_reverse_empty(self):
          self.assertEqual(reverse([]), [])

      def test_list_length(self):
          self.assertEqual(len(self.nums), 5)

      def test_list_contains(self):
          self.assertIn(3, self.nums)

      def test_list_not_contains(self):
          self.assertNotIn(6, self.nums)

      def test_list_sum(self):
          self.assertEqual(sum(self.nums), 15)

      def test_list_max(self):
          self.assertEqual(max(self.nums), 5)

Step 2 — Convert tests/test_old_style.py to pytest style in-place.
Rules:
- Remove all unittest imports and class inheritance.
- Convert setUp to pytest fixtures (use @pytest.fixture).
- Replace self.assertEqual(a, b) with assert a == b.
- Replace self.assertTrue(x) with assert x.
- Replace self.assertFalse(x) with assert not x.
- Replace self.assertIn(a, b) with assert a in b.
- Replace self.assertNotIn(a, b) with assert a not in b.
- Replace self.assertRaises(E, fn, *args) with pytest.raises(E).
- All 20 test functions must remain present (same names after removing
  class prefix is fine, e.g. test_add_positive stays test_add_positive).
- No import unittest line may remain in the file.

Requirements:
- All 20 tests must pass under pytest after conversion.
- No unittest imports or TestCase subclasses may remain.
- Fixtures must replace setUp blocks where applicable.

Three route files use function-based views with individual @app.route
decorators. Migrate all of them to Flask MethodView classes registered
with as_view(). The URL patterns and behavior must remain identical.

Step 1 — Create these 3 route files using function-based views:

routes/users.py:
  from flask import Blueprint, jsonify, request

  users_bp = Blueprint("users", __name__)

  @users_bp.route("/users", methods=["GET"])
  def list_users():
      return jsonify({"users": []})

  @users_bp.route("/users/<int:user_id>", methods=["GET"])
  def get_user(user_id):
      return jsonify({"id": user_id})

  @users_bp.route("/users", methods=["POST"])
  def create_user():
      data = request.get_json()
      return jsonify({"created": data}), 201

  @users_bp.route("/users/<int:user_id>", methods=["DELETE"])
